WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.760
Starship will attempt its first sustained orbit on Monday after 13 sub-orbital flights that kept the upper stage on passively safe paths. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 113. SpaceX plans to send Starship Flight 14 Skyward as early as 8.15 am eastern time on September 28th from starbase, Texas. The launch window runs 75 minutes and closes at 9.30 am eastern time.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:34.620
The target is a 275 kilometers altitude orbit that will let the upper stage complete six

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.760
full orbits over nearly 10 hours before it splashes down in the Pacific west of Chile.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.360
This flight gives the upper stage the full velocity increment needed for orbital insertion

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.540
instead of the suborbital paths flown on the first 13 tests.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.920
The booster will handle launch, ascent, stage separation, boost backburn and a landing

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.140
burn at a simulated offshore point in the Gulf of America.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:05.580
Coverage begins on the SpaceX website and on X around 7.45 am eastern time.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.020
The 33 Raptor engines will light once weather, fueling, and other checks clear.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.300
The mission is the 14th flight overall and the next step toward turning the vehicle into

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.820
a fully reusable system.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:24.380
According to Mashable, the upper stage will also deploy 26 Starling V3 satellites once

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.460
it reaches orbit. That deployment marks the first time Starlink has carried and released

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.860
a payload. Earlier flight stayed suborbital, so any problem would still produce a safe

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:41.400
reentry rather than an uncontrolled object in orbit. Orbital velocity sits near 7.8 kilometers

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.320
per second, which forces the vehicle to manage its own propulsion, attitude, and communications

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.820
for hours instead of riding a ballistic arc back to Earth.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.240
The upper stage measures 52 meters long and will carry its complete thermal protection

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.580
system avionics and propellant load into the 275 kilometers orbit.

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.960
Per finance, big O, the velocity change is the key distinction between simply reaching

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.440
space and actually staying in orbit.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:17.540
On the Falcon side, booster B1100 flew its 10th mission during the USS F-385 launch on

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:18.560
September 26.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:25.120
It touched down on the drone ship, of course I still love you, just 35 days after its previous flight.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:31.200
Lift off from SLC4E at Vandenberg happened at 7am Pacific time.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:39.160
Keep track reports that USSF-385 became the fourth classified space force mission from that same

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:46.480
pad in roughly six weeks. The earlier flights were USSF-366 on August 15th.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:54.880
USSF 153 on September 10th, and USSF 259 on September 17th.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.240
Payload details stay unidentified, yet the trajectory profiles line up with star-shield satellites.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.880
After the first two of those missions, the Space Force recorded 23 satellites each,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.060
while the third placed 17 into a different plane.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:17.400
KTXS notes that the rapid cadence shows how quickly national security assets are moving into orbit.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:24.300
Crew 13 astronauts reached Kennedy Space Center on Saturday. The team includes Commander Jessica

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:31.540
Watkins, Pilot Luke Delaney, Mission Specialist Joshua Kutrick, and Mission Specialist Sergei

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:37.680
Teteriatnikov. Three of the four are making their first trip to orbit and launch remains set for

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.160
no earlier than October 1st. Keep track also lists 10th Starlink-related conjunction reports on

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.820
the current Socrates list, but all carry stale or erroneous data and none meet the high-risk threshold.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.860
8th Starlink satellites currently show active decay predictions as part of routine end-of-life

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:03.140
disposal. On Reddit, the spaceflight community is marking the 10th anniversary of Elon Musk's first

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:07.420
public Mars architecture presentation at IAC 2016.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:14.440
Observers note the direct line from that early ITS concept through BFR to the starship

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.460
that may finally reach orbit tomorrow.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:22.060
Another thread on the same site looks at how the Gulf Inter Coastal Waterway could give

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.760
Starbase a sheltered barge route to the new Louisiana property.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:31.200
The eastern edge of the site connects to the waterway through the freshwater canal, where

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:32.560
Dredging has already begun.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:39.320
The FAA launch license for Flight 14 spells out the booster test objectives and confirms

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:43.420
hardware and software updates that address issues seen on the prior flight.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.060
One thing worth watching is the pending regulatory approval for the Monday Starship launch,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.580
which has already slipped once from an earlier September 22 target date.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:59.780
The FAA license remains the final gate before the vehicle can attempt sustaining orbital flight.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:06.720
On the AI front, Elon Musk stated that GROC lags behind Anthropics AI model, and that

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.860
XAI needs time to catch up.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.980
The comment came during a discussion of ongoing model development.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:19.760
Developers are also comparing Muse code, Claude code, and cursor, as AI coding agents

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.000
in 2026 evaluations.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.600
The comparison center on how each tool integrates frontier models into daily workflows.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.740
lows. Looking back over the past week, the biggest threads have been star links pushed

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:38.160
toward a single-price global mobile service. Raptor engine production scaling at McGregor,

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:45.520
the 7,000 acre star-based expansion, new Pentagon tracking data for collision avoidance, Google's

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:52.120
Orbital AI satellite test, and the four star-shield launches in 43 days. Each of those threads

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.520
continues to move forward with new hardware or data this week.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.580
The engineering reality behind Flight 14 is the shift from sub-orbital to orbital velocity

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:07.520
for the upper stage. Reaching 7.8 kilometers per second means the vehicle must now manage

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.840
active propulsion, attitude control, and communications for nearly 10 hours across six orbits,

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.120
instead of relying on a ballistic arc that naturally returns it to Earth.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:25.040
The 52 meter upper stage carries its full thermal protection system, avionics and propellant

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:31.660
reserves into a stable 275 kilometers orbit, where any loss of control would create a long

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:36.280
lived object whose reentry timing and location become far harder to predict.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:42.100
Prior flights deliberately avoided this regime to keep the test envelope passively safe while

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:47.300
while the team iterated on Raptor performance, heat shield attachment, and stage separation.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:53.740
Adding the first payload deployment of 26 V3 satellites further requires the upper stage

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:59.220
to execute a precise deployment sequence before the deorbit burn that targets splash-down

00:06:59.220 --> 00:06:59.980
west of Chile.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:06.300
The booster's role remains a simulated offshore landing, preserving the focus on upper-stage

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.400
orbital operations that unlock the next phase of rapid reusability.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.560
The decision to fly the full orbital profile also changes how the team must think about margins.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:22.300
In sub-orbital tests, the vehicle could tolerate a late anomaly because gravity would still

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.080
pull it back on a safe trajectory.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.260
Once the upper stage holds 7.8 kilometers per second, it must keep its own propulsion,

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.740
guidance, and thermal systems healthy for the entire duration of six orbits.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:40.000
That requirement drives the hardware and software changes already incorporated into

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.180
flight 14. The 33 Raptor engines on the booster will still perform their standard ascent and

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.100
boost back sequence, but the real test now sits with the upper state's ability to stay

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:56.200
under control while it deploys satellites and then executes a controlled reentry after

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:02.220
almost 10 hours in space. This single change in velocity regime therefore forces every

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:08.340
subsystem to demonstrate reliability that previous flights never had to prove. The economics

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:13.920
of reaching that reliability, follow the same first principles pattern SpaceX has used on Falcon.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.220
Raw materials for a starship upper stage are measured in tons of stainless steel,

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:23.700
kilograms of heat shield tiles, and thousands of liters of propellant.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.000
The finished article costs many times that raw material floor because of the precision

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:34.760
manufacturing, testing, and integration required to survive repeated thermal cycles and engine

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:41.380
fireings. Flight 14 begins the process of collapsing that multiplier by proving the vehicle can

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:48.040
operate for hours in orbit and then return intact. Each successful orbit adds data that reduces

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:53.480
the uncertainty around long-duration control, thermal management, and payload deployment.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.040
Over successive flights, the gap between raw inputs and finished capability narrows,

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:07.560
which is the same mechanism that turned Falcon 9 from an expensive prototype into a vehicle that can fly more than 10 times on the same booster.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:14.880
Before we go, keep an eye on the final FAA sign-off that will clear flight 14 for its Monday attempt.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.480
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

